title: Hide private relay recipients from authenticated peer discovery
updated_at: "2026-07-21T13:50:17Z"
---
## Purpose

Remove global discovery of private relay recipients and operational metadata while preserving deliberate public discovery surfaces.

## Work

- Change production peer listing so authentication alone does not enumerate private aliases, session IDs, host IDs, keys, versions, OS data, lease timing, or reservation metadata.
- Define owner/admin visibility and capability-authorised resolution exactly as approved in the protocol design.
- Keep public/gated/unlisted room discovery semantics explicit and prevent room endpoints from becoming a side channel for private recipient metadata.
- Make enumeration-resistant errors and timing behaviour practical; avoid distinguishable “unknown recipient” versus “known but unauthorised” responses where the design requires privacy.
- Preserve explicit development observability only behind unmistakable dev configuration.

## Acceptance evidence

- Authenticated unauthorised peers cannot list, resolve, or infer a private recipient through list, send errors, room APIs, lease metadata, or related endpoints.
- Owners/admins and deliberately public surfaces retain only the intended visibility.
- In-memory/SQLite and HTTP route-auth matrix tests cover the new policy.

## B261 post-B219 constraints

Implement explicit private/owner/admin/public discovery views at the backend/domain seam, covering `list_peers`, pubkey lookup, registration metadata, and route/error side channels. Do not rely on response-only redaction or alter deliberate room visibility beyond DM-route leakage.